<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Guinea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> is the north-eastern part of the tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> off West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, stretching roughly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a> in Liberia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lopez">Cape Lopez</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>. Its coasts include the former Gold, Slave and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coasts</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, and it was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. Today it is a major source of oil and cocoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea.jpg" alt="Gulf of Guinea" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Guinea_(English).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> is the north-easternmost part of the tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lopez">Cape Lopez</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> north and west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a> in Liberia. Null Island, defined as the intersection of the Equator and the Prime Meridian, lies within it. Many rivers drain into the gulf, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, the main river, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>, and its coastline includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a> and the Bight of Bonny.</p><p>The largest of its islands form a south-west to north-east chain that is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> line of volcanoes. They include Annobón, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> and Corisco, all belonging to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a>, and Bobowasi Island, which belongs to the Western Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. São Tomé and Príncipe, two islands about 140 kilometres apart that are part of an extinct volcanic range, became an independent nation in 1975 after Portuguese rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The origin of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> is disputed. Some have said it is a corrupt form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, but the historian Bovill finds this unacceptable, since the name was in use in the Maghrib and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> long before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry</a>'s time. It appears as Gunuia on a map of about 1320 by the Genoese cartographer Giovanni di Carignano and as Ginyia in the Catalan atlas of 1375. A passage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> suggests it may be a corrupt form of Jenne, a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> long famed as a market and seat of learning. Bovill thinks it more probable that it derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> aguinaou, meaning Negro.</p><p>The modern use of the name for the coast dates only from 1481, when the Portuguese built the fort of São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and King John II was permitted to style himself Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. Today the word appears in the names of Guinea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of Guinea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The federation covered about 4.7 million square kilometres, from the Atlantic to Lake Chad and from the Sahara to the <b>Gulf of Guinea</b>, with a population of well over ten million that approached twenty million by the late 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The word gorilla comes from Punic: Hanno the Navigator’s interpreters gave the name to a tribe of hostile ‘hairy people’ in the <b>Gulf of Guinea</b>, it passed into Greek as gorillai, and in 1847 Thomas S. Savage applied it to the western gorilla.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1956 the colony became the Province of the <b>Gulf of Guinea</b>, and in 1959 a law declared Guineans equal in rights to Spaniards.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-guinea">Spanish Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
